import QuickFact from "../../../components/QuickFact.astro";
---

<Article
  title="Gloomhaven vs Jaws of the Lion: Which Should You Buy First?"
  description="The two boxes play the same game at completely different sizes. Here's how to pick."
  game="gloomhaven"
  gameTitle="Gloomhaven"
  category="Buying Guide"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    This is the most common question in the entire Gloomhaven world, and it has a
    clear answer for most people. Both boxes run the same combat system, the same
    card mechanics, the same feel. What differs is scale, price, and how much work
    the game does to teach you.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Buy Jaws of the Lion first. It's cheaper, teaches you the system through play instead of a rulebook, and sets up in a fraction of the time. Move to Gloomhaven only once you know you love it." />

  <h2>The Short Version</h2>

  <ComparisonTable title="Jaws of the Lion vs Gloomhaven">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th></th>
          <th>Jaws of the Lion</th>
          <th>Gloomhaven</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Price</strong></td>
          <td>Roughly a third as much</td>
          <td>Premium, and heavy shipping</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Scenarios</strong></td>
          <td>25</td>
          <td>95+</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Characters</strong></td>
          <td>4, all available immediately</td>
          <td>17, six to start, the rest unlocked</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Maps</strong></td>
          <td>Printed in a book — no assembly</td>
          <td>Cardboard tiles you build each scenario</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Setup per scenario</strong></td>
          <td>A few minutes</td>
          <td>Often 15–30 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Learning</strong></td>
          <td>Guided tutorial across the first scenarios</td>
          <td>A large rulebook, read up front</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Campaign length</strong></td>
          <td>Tens of hours</td>
          <td>Well over a hundred hours</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Standalone?</strong></td>
          <td>Yes — needs nothing else</td>
          <td>Yes</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ComparisonTable>

  <h2>Why Jaws of the Lion Wins for Most People</h2>

  <div class="my-6 space-y-3">
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">DECISIVE</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">It teaches you by playing</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">The first several scenarios introduce rules a few at a time, in play, with no rulebook to read first. Gloomhaven hands you a substantial manual and expects you to absorb it before scenario one. For a group where one person does all the reading, this difference is enormous.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">DECISIVE</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">The scenario book kills setup time</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">You play directly on printed map pages. No hunting for tile
      G1b, no rebuilding a room because you misread the layout. This one change is why Jaws of the Lion campaigns actually get finished and Gloomhaven campaigns so often stall.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 bg-white rounded-xl border border-bark/10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-1">
        <span class="px-2 py-0.5 bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 text-[11px] font-bold rounded-full">DECISIVE</span>
        <h4 class="font-heading font-semibold text-sm text-bark">It's a small bet on a big commitment</h4>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-bark/60">Gloomhaven asks for a lot of money, a lot of shelf space and a hundred-plus hours from a group that has to keep showing up. Jaws of the Lion answers "will we actually enjoy this?" for a third of the price, and its 25 scenarios are a complete campaign in their own right.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>When to Go Straight to Gloomhaven</h2>

  <p>
    The case isn't zero. Buy the big box first if:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>You already know you love this kind of game.</strong> If you've played a tactical campaign crawler and finished it, you don't need convincing — and Jaws of the Lion will feel small.</li>
    <li><strong>You want the character variety.</strong> Four characters versus seventeen is the single biggest gap between the boxes. Unlocking a new class is one of Gloomhaven's real pleasures, and Jaws of the Lion barely offers it.</li>
    <li><strong>Your group is stable and committed.</strong> The long campaign is the point. If you have four people who'll reliably meet for a year, the bigger box rewards that.</li>
    <li><strong>You'd resent buying twice.</strong> There's meaningful overlap in the core system, and some people would rather pay once.</li>
  </ul>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="Don't underestimate the physical size">
    Gloomhaven is one of the heaviest mass-market board games ever produced — a
    box you need both hands and a plan for. Check your shelf before you order, and
    be honest about whether you'll want to lift it down on a weeknight.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>What About Frosthaven?</h2>

  <p>
    Frosthaven is the full sequel to Gloomhaven: more scenarios, more characters,
    and an entire outpost-building layer where you gather resources, craft, and
    manage a settlement through seasons between missions.
  </p>

  <p>
    It's excellent, and it is emphatically <strong>not</strong> a starting point.
    It's bigger, heavier and more complex than Gloomhaven in every dimension, and
    it assumes you're already fluent in the system. Treat it as the third box you
    buy, not the first.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="The sensible order">
    Jaws of the Lion → Gloomhaven → Frosthaven. Each step up assumes comfort with
    the one before, and each adds scale rather than changing what the game
    fundamentally is.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>A Note on Second Edition</h2>

  <p>
    Gloomhaven has been reissued in a revised second edition that tidies rules
    ambiguities, improves the components and smooths some of the original's rough
    edges. If you're buying the big box new, it's the version to look for — the
    first edition's quirks were real, and most of the community's house rules
    existed to patch them.
  </p>

  <p>
    It doesn't change the recommendation above. Second edition is a better
    Gloomhaven; it isn't a gentler one.
  </p>

  <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

  <p>
    Jaws of the Lion for almost everyone. It is the same game, taught better, at a
    price that doesn't hurt if your group turns out not to want it. If you finish
    all 25 scenarios and want more, the big box will still be there — and you'll
    approach it already knowing every rule.
  </p>

  <p>
    Before committing either way, it's worth understanding
    <a href="/games/gloomhaven/how-gloomhaven-works/">how the card system actually works</a>
    and <a href="/games/gloomhaven/how-long-does-gloomhaven-take/">how much time a scenario really takes</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
